course: dictionary
lesson: 15032
title: Restructure: organising things in a new way
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: To restructure something means to organise it in a new way. Teams, clubs and plans can all be restructured. «The school restructured its library so the picture books were now by the door.» The school did not buy new books. It changed how the books were arranged.
explain: You can also restructure a sentence. «Yesterday, the dog ran into the park.» can be restructured as «Into the park ran the dog yesterday.» The words are nearly the same, but the order is different. Writers do this to make a sentence clearer or more exciting.
explain: Be careful with this word in the news. «The company is restructuring» often means its jobs are being changed, and some people may lose their jobs. Adults sometimes use this long word to sound less harsh. When you are unsure, look at the words around it and check a dictionary.
fact: Restructure is a verb: to restructure, it restructures, it restructured, it is restructuring.
fact: «Restructure» means to organise something in a new way.
fact: You can restructure a company, a team, a plan or a sentence.
fact: «Reorganise» means nearly the same thing and is used in everyday speech.
fact: In business news, «restructuring» can mean jobs are changed or lost, so read the words around it carefully.
exercise: The team decided to restructure its plan so that each person had a clear job. What does restructure mean here? | To paint the plan in bright colours | To lose the plan on the bus | *To organise the plan in a new way | To copy the plan exactly as it was
exercise: Which is a restructured version of «Yesterday, the dog ran into the park.»? | *Into the park ran the dog yesterday. | The dog ran park into yesterday. | The park ran into the dog yesterday.
exercise: Dad says, «The company is restructuring, so some jobs may change.» What does restructuring mean here? | Painting the office walls | Buying a new computer for every person in town | Going on holiday for a week | *Organising its work in a new way, which can change or remove jobs
test: Which word means nearly the same as restructure in everyday speech? | Shout | Forget | *Reorganise | Sleep
test: Which word is the past tense of restructure? | Restructure | Restructures | *Restructured | Restructuring
exam: What does it mean to restructure a sentence? | To cross it out completely | *To change the order or form of its words | To read it aloud slowly | To write it in capital letters only
